      <version name="1.2.0" author="Eric Avondo" time="2026-08-30T19:30:53Z">
        <changelog><![CDATA[Panels now carry up to 100 elements instead of 50. Existing panels and their links are unaffected: the serialized stream keeps a frozen tier per version, so a panel saved by an earlier build is read back with its 50 elements, its labels and its parameter links intact, and the space above simply comes up empty. The shared-memory map was re-laid to give each track room for 100 elements. Two panels on the same track no longer share one set of link cells, where the second used to wipe the first: each panel now gets its own, and the first panel of a track keeps the address it had, so an earlier project finds its links where it left them. The Panel Builder is now part of the package. Select a track, run it, pick a plugin from the chain: it lists the parameters, you tick the ones you want, and it drops a finished panel on the track -- elements typed, placed, named, coloured and already linked to the plugin, without a single Learn to run. Every parameter the plugin declares is listed, none is hidden on its name; a radio button is laid down only where the source control really enumerates its positions, and a knob with detents stays a knob. It needs the ReaImGui extension for its window, and says so plainly if it is missing. The MIDI CC a control sends is now a plain one-way output: the service no longer sweeps every parameter of every plugin on the track looking for a matching MIDI learn, which was by far its heaviest task and paired up links nobody had asked for. A control driven by CC therefore no longer follows the plugin back, and shows no value pop-up -- Direct Link is the bidirectional route. A link does not silence the CC: a control can drive one plugin through Direct Link and another through MIDI learn at the same time, as before. A panel element is an ordinary FX parameter, so REAPER's own MIDI learn binds it to a physical knob like any other, through the global control path -- no armed track and no MIDI input routing -- which composes with Direct Link into a full chain from a hardware controller to a plugin parameter; the manual now says so. Restart REAPER after updating -- the shared-memory map has moved, and a panel or service still running from the previous build addresses the new one's cells until it is reloaded.]]></changelog>
